Peyronie's Disease
Genitourinary Renal & Mens Health
Hard fibrous penile plaque, Severe penile curvature during erection, Painful erections

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GRM-031
Reason for Visit: Acute severe penile pain and profound psychological distress following the sudden discovery of a structural deformity during an erection.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient in extreme distress experiencing severe localized penile pain and acute curvature during an erection, raising the immediate concern for a penile fracture (rupture of the tunica albuginea). Urgent after-hours assessment was critically necessary to definitively rule out a surgical emergency requiring immediate operative repair, and to provide targeted analgesia, preventing further tissue damage or psychological decompensation before regular clinic hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My penis suddenly bent sharply to the left during an erection tonight, and the pain was so sharp I thought it had snapped.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports the insidious development of mild penile discomfort over the past month, which sharply escalated tonight. They describe a sudden, pronounced unnatural curvature and severe bending of the penis occurring specifically during an erection. This was accompanied by severe localized pain, characteristic of the early inflammatory phase of the disease. They noted a sudden shortening of the penile shaft and an hourglass deformity. The intense pain and mechanical deformity have resulted in immediate erectile dysfunction and absolute inability to achieve vaginal intromission. Crucially, they report a complete absence of pain when the penis returned to a flaccid state.
Functional Status: Physically independent, but currently pacing in extreme anxiety due to the fear of permanent genital damage.
Social/Safety Concerns: High acute psychological distress and fear of permanent loss of sexual function.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/85 | HR: 98 | Temp: 36.7C | RR: 18 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Visibly anxious, sweating slightly, frequently asking for reassurance regarding the physical integrity of their genitalia.
Physical Exam: Genitourinary: The penis is currently flaccid and painless to gentle handling. Deep palpation reveals a dense, hard fibrous plaque within the tunica albuginea on the left dorsal aspect of the penile shaft. There is no large, expanding hematoma or "eggplant" deformity visible. The penile vascular supply appears normal, with intact distal sensation. Expected investigation results would include visualization of dense, calcified hyperechoic plaques within the tunica albuginea on a targeted penile duplex ultrasound.
Environmental Observations: Clean environment; the patient is wearing loose clothing to avoid any friction.
Medication Reconciliation: No medications taken prior to the visit.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Peyronie's Disease - Status: Exacerbated (Acute Inflammatory Phase)
Differentials:

Penile Fracture - Ruled out/unlikely due to the complete absence of a rapid, massive hematoma (eggplant sign) and the lack of a distinct popping sound preceding immediate detumescence; the structural plaque is palpable.

Priapism - Ruled out/unlikely due to the current completely flaccid state and the fact that the pain occurred during a normal, stimulus-driven erection that subsequently resolved.

Sexually Transmitted Infection - Ruled out/unlikely due to the strict absence of urethral discharge, dysuria, or mucosal ulcerations.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient experienced a terrifying acute pain event and structural deformity during an erection, strongly mimicking a catastrophic penile fracture. An urgent after-hours physical examination was absolutely mandatory to differentiate a chronic, inflammatory plaque formation (Peyronie's disease) from an acute rupture of the tunica albuginea, which necessitates immediate emergency surgery to prevent permanent erectile failure. Confirming the absence of an expanding hematoma and the presence of a localized fibrous plaque allowed for immediate, definitive reassurance, halting the severe panic attack and safely diverting the patient from an unnecessary emergency department presentation.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed oral NSAIDs (Ibuprofen 400mg) to be taken regularly for the next week to suppress the acute inflammatory phase of the plaque formation. Provided extensive reassurance that the penis is not broken and that the condition is a recognized, manageable inflammatory process. Ordered a routine outpatient penile duplex ultrasound.
Positive Behavioral Change: To prevent further micro-trauma to the tunica albuginea during the active inflammatory phase, the patient is strictly advised to abstain from all sexual intercourse and vigorous masturbation for the next four weeks. Experiencing a steady reduction in the resting ache by maintaining strict pelvic rest will positively reinforce this necessary period of physical abstinence, allowing the acute inflammation to safely subside.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None required.
DME/Safety (Optional): None required.
Follow-up: Routine GP follow-up in two weeks to review the ultrasound and discuss potential specialist urology referral for plaque stabilization therapies.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate Emergency Department presentation is required if the patient develops a prolonged, painful erection lasting more than four hours, or if they experience sudden, massive swelling and dark bruising of the entire penile shaft.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The clinical exam strongly points to a fibrous plaque, but formal ultrasound imaging is required to map the exact extent of the scar tissue.
Clinical Course: The acute pain upon erection will slowly improve as the active inflammation settles, though the curvature will likely remain and stabilize over the next 12 to 18 months.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

If your penis suddenly swells massively and turns dark purple, or if you develop an erection that will not go down after four hours, you must seek immediate emergency medical care.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A tough, fibrous scar has formed inside the lining of your penis. When you get an erection, the normal tissue expands, but the scar tissue does not, causing the sharp pain and the severe bending. It is not broken, and you are not bleeding internally. The anti-inflammatory medication will help calm the active irritation in the scar.
Actionable Steps: Take the anti-inflammatory medication with food. Strictly avoid any sexual activity or bending of the penis for the next month to prevent worsening the internal tear.

